            <ArticleTitle>Defining morbid obesity in children based on BMI 40 at age 18 using the extended international (IOTF) cut-offs.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e94-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.2047-6310.2014.00217.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Studies have reported that children who are obese are becoming more severely obese.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">We aimed to classify obese children based on age- and gender-specific centile curves passing through body mass index (BMI) 30, 35 and 40 at age 18 as 'class I', 'class II' or severe, and 'class III' or morbid obesity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">In addition to the International Obesity Task Force BMI cut-offs corresponding to BMI 30 and 35, we calculated the BMI cut-offs corresponding to BMI 40 using the LMS method proposed by Cole and Lobstein. We classified 217 obese children according to these criteria.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Fifty-six (25.8%) children had class III obesity, 73 (33.6%) class II obesity and 88 (40.6%) class I obesity. Class III obese children had a higher waist circumference, systolic blood pressure and fasting insulinaemia compared with less obese children.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">It is clinically important to classify obese children in different classes of obesity severity.</AbstractText>
